<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="instrument_render_determinism">

  <!-- Proposal 37 P3c, AC1: A RENDER IS ONE RUN, WHATEVER HAPPENED BEFORE IT.

       An instrument is the only CLASS-1 component in the graph: a synth voice
       carries its envelope, its filter poles and its portamento across page
       boundaries, so what a page SOUNDS like can depend on what the processor
       was doing when the page started. Playback and a render start at different
       positions and freeze different pages, so a render made AFTER playback in
       the same process could continue the playback run's voices and produce
       different bytes from the same render in a fresh process (design F4, the
       determinism hole). `SApplication::beginRun(pos)` closes it on the MAIN
       thread, before the run's first demand: every instrument track gets
       `forgetContinuity()` and `invalidateRenderPathRange(pos, INT64_MAX)`.

       WHAT THIS CASE MEASURES TODAY, STATED HONESTLY. On the engine as it
       stands the case ALSO passes with the barrier removed, and that was
       verified rather than assumed (both call sites disabled; det_a and det_b
       still compared byte-identical, 1 728 044 bytes). The reason is P3b's
       recorded known debt: the scheduler renders each instrument page TWICE,
       and the second call at the same position is never contiguous with the
       first, so EVERY instrument page in every consumer is produced by the
       reposition path - reset + chase + pre-roll K - which is a pure function
       of the page's own start position and the events. Position-pure pages are
       trivially run-independent, so nothing can leak between runs to be caught.
       That is a property of a debt that proposal 20's pipelining work is meant
       to remove; the moment an instrument page is served from a CONTINUOUS
       chain, the run it chained from starts to matter and this case is what
       says so. It is a REGRESSION gate on a guarantee, not a reproduction of a
       live bug - which is why the PR body and STATE.md say the same thing.

       THE FIXTURE is the in-repo `tw.native.303`: linked in, so this case runs
       in a worktree with no submodules, and it is the STATEFUL instrument. The
       CLAP/VST3 sine fixtures have no envelope at all, so a spliced run would
       produce identical bytes and the case would be vacuous by construction.
       The 303 has a decay envelope, a resonant ladder whose four poles are
       integrators, a rectangular VCA with a 6 ms release and portamento, and it
       is MONOPHONIC - the short notes below take the held note's voice over and
       slide back to it on their note-offs. Every one of those is run state.

       120 BPM / 960 ppq => 1920 ticks per second.

           key 48 (C3)   tick     0  dur 11520   held, 0 - 6 s
           key 60 (C4)   tick   960  dur   240   0.5   - 0.625 s
           key 63 (D#4)  tick  2880  dur   240   1.5   - 1.625 s
           key 67 (G4)   tick  4800  dur   240   2.5   - 2.625 s
           key 55 (G3)   tick  9600  dur   240   5.0   - 5.125 s

       PLAYBACK STARTS AT 262144 (5.461 s), which is a PAGE boundary and is
       5.46 s into a note held since 0. Both halves matter: the pre-roll K is
       min(max(4096, tailFrames, P - noteStart), 4 s) = 192000 there, i.e. the
       four-second CAP bites and the chase rebuilds the held note 4 s before the
       page instead of at its real note-on. That is the one configuration in
       which a repositioned page is NOT the state a continuous run would have
       had, so it is the configuration a leak would show in.

       durationSec IS PINNED at 7.0 rather than left to the arrangement: the
       303's tailFrames() follows its `decay` parameter, and a length that moved
       with a parameter would turn a byte compare into a length compare.

       NOT ASSERTED HERE: render-vs-playback byte identity. They are different
       runs from different start positions and never were identical for a
       stateful insert (F4); the barrier makes RENDERS agree, not renders and
       playback.

       The CROSS-PROCESS half of AC1 and all of AC2c are driven by
       tests/run_xproc_determinism.cmake, which runs this case and then
       tests/cases/xproc/instrument_render_determinism_xproc.qxa into the SAME
       output directory, so the second process can name det_a.wav as its
       reference. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="tw" uid="tw.native.303"
                   name="Smaragd 303" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="" nIn="0" nOut="1"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="0" present="1" format="tw"
                            uid="tw.native.303" mode="MonoSpread"
                            state="Active" hasFeed="1"/>

    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="0" timePos="0" duration="11520" name="Held"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="0"    dur="11520" key="48" velocity="90" channel="0"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="960"  dur="240"   key="60" velocity="90" channel="0"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="2880" dur="240"   key="63" velocity="90" channel="0"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="4800" dur="240"   key="67" velocity="90" channel="0"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="9600" dur="240"   key="55" velocity="90" channel="0"/>

    <!-- A: the reference. A fresh process, nothing has played, the render is
         the first run this process makes. -->
    <render filename="det_a.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="7.0"/>

    <!-- The project is AUDIBLE. Without this a barrier that silenced the
         instrument outright would make every byte compare below pass. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="det_a.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="288000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.02" maxRms="1.0"/>

    <!-- Real playback, on the capture backend, from a page boundary deep
         inside the held note. This is what leaves the processor's lastEnd_
         pointing somewhere the render does not start, and its voices in
         mid-flight. -->
    <set-locator position="262144"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead position="327680" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>

    <!-- B: the same render, same process, after that. -->
    <render filename="det_b.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="7.0"/>

    <assert-file-identical actual="det_b.wav" expected="det_a.wav"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
